"gbone" <no@no.com> wrote:

> Does anyone have a good strategy for playing the
> wizard class?  I've tried just spellcasting and
> using non-metalic armour but it seems that
> creatures just seem to hit way to often.

Take Terry Pratchett's Rincewind as your role model:
you need to have two goals: acquire an invincible
pet, and earn a reputation as a world class
sprinter. As Rincewind more or less expressed it:
looking backwards to see if the monsters are
catching up only slows you down, the important thing
is just to run.

In more usable terms, stay out of melee, and treat
every monster as a potential instakill possessor.

Stay unburdened, it is pretty much impossible to
limit melee encounters while burdened.

Exercise with every boulder you find, to improve
your strength. Eventually, you're going to want to
be wearing Gauntlets of Dexterity, not Gauntlets of
Power, so you need to be constantly exercising your
natural strength to improve your unassisted carrying
capability.

> My recent stragety seems to more effective, which
> is to just play like a fighter class and load up
> on all types of armour.  Does any have any hints
> or tips on playing this class?

This works well for lots of people, but it isn't a
long term strategy, just one to get you up to the
point of having good armor that doesn't interfere
with spell casting: well enchanted robe, shirt,
leather or dragon scale armor, speed boots, a
helpful helm, lots of good intrinsics.

Using a wizard long-term as a combat wombat wastes
the class's best characteristics: fast spell power
recovery and no-hunger spellcasting.

_Use_ your spells, obsessively. Being always maxed
on spell power points does nothing for you, while
practicing spells helps you raise your proficiency
spell class by spell class.

Magic Missile is your friend.

A quiver of daggers is a good backup, and the only
weapon you really need to advance early on in the
game.

Seek out protection, which can be done with or
without choosing the protection racket as your
strategy.

In the longer term, wizards are one of the few
classes worth deliberately taking above XL 14. Many
correspondents here recommend XL 30 as a target,
which in practice means eating wraith corpses and
quaffing gain level potions.

